          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "Ankle swelling for someone with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction who is already taking four heart-failure medicines is considered \"routine\" in terms of immediate risk (diagnostic, coding-heart-failure-i50, condition-heart-failure, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet, guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). However, this classification is not a clearance, as the risk assessment table covers a fixed list of presentations. It is advised that if symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, medical advice should be sought (diagnostic, coding-heart-failure-i50, condition-heart-failure, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet, guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nRegarding medications, current guidance describes four drug classes as \"foundational\" for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (diagnostic, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy; research, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy). These classes are typically started together or in quick succession, rather than one by one over several months (research, Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\").\n\nThis therapy specifically applies to heart failure where the ejection fraction is 40 percent or below (diagnostic, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy; research, Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\"). Applying the same approach to heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (50 percent or above) or mildly reduced ejection fraction (41 to 49 percent) can be incorrect, because some of these drug classes may not show the same benefits in those groups (research, Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
